1 GOSUB
    The GOSUB command calls a subroutine at a label of the same command
    procedure, and RETURN comes back to the command after the GOSUB.
    The subroutine runs at the same command level: it sees the
    procedure's labels and local symbols.

    Syntax:

        GOSUB label
        RETURN [status]

    GOSUBs may be nested 15 deep.  RETURN's status, an expression, becomes
    $STATUS, and its message is shown if it's a failure, as EXIT's is;
    without one, $STATUS is %X10000001.  A label that isn't found ends
    the procedure (DCL-W-USGOSUB), and a RETURN with no GOSUB is
    DCL-W-BADRET.  At the terminal, RETURN shows nothing: with a status,
    $STATUS becomes it, and without one $STATUS is marked as shown.

    Example:

        $ GOSUB SHOW_COUNT
        $ EXIT
        $ SHOW_COUNT:
        $ SHOW SYMBOL COUNT
        $ RETURN
